<Base title="About">
  <div class="about">
    <h1>About Problem Atlas</h1>
    <p>Problem Atlas collects the evidence on India's biggest problems in one place, and puts the solutions being tried right next to them, so readers can move from "what is wrong" to "what works".</p>
    <h2>How sources are chosen</h2>
    <p>We prefer government statistics, peer-reviewed research, reports from established institutions, and reporting from reputable outlets.</p>
    <h2>How we cite</h2>
    <p>Every claim on this site links to where it comes from. The summary at the top of each page lists its sources, every point under causes, impact, how it works and evidence carries a numbered note that leads to the source list at the bottom of the page, and every statistic and chart names its source.</p>
    <p>We only cite a page after opening it and checking that it says what we report. Where a source is a news report or summary of a study, the note says so. If you find a claim that does not match its source, please tell us.</p>
    <h2>Going global</h2>
    <p>Every entry is tagged with a region. India comes first; other countries will be added region by region, and the same problems can then be compared across countries.</p>
    <h2>Suggest a resource</h2>
    <p>A contribution form is planned. It will let anyone suggest an article, paper or video for review.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
